Let bystander block refs with a full page image through the redo filter.

The bystander filter from the walredo patch returns BLK_NOTFOUND for
any block the request did not ask for, so records touching pages
outside the batch cannot fault in state the worker does not have.
That breaks on multi block full page image records. log_newpage_range
packs up to 32 pages into a single XLOG_FPI record during index
builds, and with data checksums on by default in 18 the same shape
shows up as XLOG_FPI_FOR_HINT. The record lands in the chain of every
page it covers, so a batch asking for one of them replays a record
carrying images for 31 bystanders, and the FPI redo routine (and the
gin and hash ones) insist on BLK_RESTORED for every image a record
carries. Getting BLK_NOTFOUND instead is a FATAL in the worker and a
failed read for the backend.

Restoring an image is safe for a bystander: RestoreBlockImage
overwrites the whole page without reading what was there, so no half
replayed state can leak into a page the worker never materialized.
So the filter now only swallows bystander refs that carry no
applyable image.

diff --git a/src/backend/access/transam/xlogutils.c b/src/backend/access/transam/xlogutils.c
index a4e4f2a..ace70ad 100644
--- a/src/backend/access/transam/xlogutils.c
+++ b/src/backend/access/transam/xlogutils.c
@@ -379,9 +379,16 @@ XLogReadBufferForRedoExtended(XLogReaderState *record,
 	 * or write into them; their own requests replay these same records
 	 * against their full history.  Init references pass through, because
 	 * redo routines expect a valid buffer from an init and the page they
-	 * build cannot be read again past this filter.
+	 * build cannot be read again past this filter.  A reference carrying
+	 * an image to apply passes through too: the restore below overwrites
+	 * the whole page without reading it, so no half replayed state can
+	 * leak in, and redo routines like the XLOG_FPI one insist on
+	 * BLK_RESTORED for every image a record carries.  log_newpage_range
+	 * packs up to 32 pages into one such record, so a batch routinely
+	 * sees images for blocks it never asked for.
 	 */
 	if (zou_redo_active && !willinit &&
+		!XLogRecBlockImageApply(record, block_id) &&
 		zou_redo_bystander(rlocator, forknum, blkno))
 	{
 		*buf = InvalidBuffer;
